{"kind":"request","text":"codex: design how a person plays chess from the browser. Hugh asked for this. I read the current surface first, and the gap is narrower and more interesting than it looks, so this asks for a design rather than an implementation.\n\nThis replaces my c58f5049, which I retired at 08563de8 because I rested it on a retired artifact and filed it stale from birth. Same substance, live bases.\n\nWHAT ALREADY EXISTS, which surprised me. The browser is already a signing party. app.js generates an Ed25519 keypair with window.crypto.subtle.generateKey({name: \"Ed25519\"}, false, [\"sign\",\"verify\"]), sends the public key to /v1/live/session/prepare, signs the returned challenge, and opens a session. Chat submissions are signed the same way. Challenge-response, fingerprints and per-game session actors are all built and working.\n\nWHAT IS MISSING, precisely. Two things, and only two.\n\nFirst, the browser key is ephemeral. It is generated fresh into a module-scoped variable each time a session opens, with extractable false, so nothing persists and nothing can be exported. That is exactly right for a leased presence identity and exactly wrong for a seat: a durable seat binds to an actor fingerprint, and this fingerprint dies on reload.\n\nSecond, the web surface never writes durably. runServe builds newReadHandler; there is no openWriter, no host append, and no signing of a durable intent anywhere in web.go. Every POST under /v1/live is presence, chat or motion, and the code says so plainly — motion hints live only in leased presence and disappear on renewal, revocation or expiry. So the UI can show the board, list legal moves, and show your opponent the drag in flight, but the move is committed by chess move at the command line. That is the whole of the gap.\n\nTHE SHAPE I THINK IS RIGHT, offered as a starting point rather than an instruction. The host package says any syntactically valid act carrying a good signature over a well-formed intent is admitted, and that the submit path is open to whoever holds a key. The browser holds a key. So the natural design is that the browser key becomes a real chess actor: it joins a seat durably, signs its own move intents locally, and the server relays the signed act without ever holding a player private key. A non-extractable CryptoKey can be persisted in IndexedDB, so the key survives reloads while the private half never leaves the browser and never reaches the server. The server gains no new authority, which means the loopback restriction keeps meaning exactly what it means today.\n\nWHY A DESIGN FIRST. That shape moves a trust boundary. Today the serve process cannot forge a move because it holds no key, and whatever is built must not quietly give it one. And browser-side intent encoding is the kind of duplication that fails silently: two encoders that agree in testing and diverge on one edge case put a bad signature in a permanent log. Both deserve deciding before either is written.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT ASKING FOR. Not a change to the fold, not a relaxation of the loopback or trusted-host rules, and not a stopgap where the browser posts a move for the CLI to sign later. If a server-held key is genuinely the better design, say so and argue it — I would rather be shown wrong than have my sketch built because I was the one who filed it.","body":{"conditions":"Produce a design, not an implementation. Answer, against the code as it stands: (1) Identity. Whether the browser's Ed25519 key becomes a durable chess actor holding a seat in its own right, or whether delegation to a server-held key is proposed instead; state which and why. If the browser key is the actor, say how it persists across reloads while staying non-extractable, and what happens when it is lost, because a seat bound to a key nobody holds is a game nobody can finish. (2) The submit path. How a browser-signed move reaches the log, what the server does with it, and an explicit statement of whether the serve process ever holds or uses a player private key. If it does not, say what prevents a hostile page from having the server sign for it, since that is the property the read-only surface gets for free today and would be spending. (3) Intent encoding in the browser. A durable act needs exact signed bytes, and the browser today signs only live challenge bytes the server supplies. Say how a move intent is encoded, who owns that encoding's correctness, and how a divergence between browser and server encodings is caught by a test rather than by a bad signature in a permanent log. (4) Seat binding. How joining a seat from the UI works given SeatFor keys seats by actor fingerprint, and whether a seat joined from the CLI can be played from a browser or only a browser-joined one. (5) Scope boundary. What stays out: at minimum no change to the loopback restriction, no weakening of TrustedHostHandler, no change to the fold. Record Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions, and treat the browser as untrusted input in the security section even though it is same-origin. No code changes in this lane: the design lands as a note with an exact-head artifact and independent review, and implementation becomes its own request after hugh ratifies the design. Write no initiative or type tags; proposal 5409bc64 is unratified.","severity":"normal","to":"09c97907402e7747d051c0c599d9eb463c48d19825820a3e4c433cde2e60e2a8"}}